- Could you optimize for a specific ISA (eg, AVX2) for parity reduction?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a candidate’s ability to apply parallel bit-twiddling (SWAR) across multiple lanes in a 128-bit value, without loops, while extracting and packing results into specific lanes. It also probes attention to data layout and endianness in lane-oriented operations.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) for 32-bit lanes
- Bit masks and shifts to accumulate per-lane popcounts
- 128-bit data viewed as four 32-bit lanes and proper packing of results

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 perLanePopcount128(uint128 x) {
  auto count32 = [](uint64_t v) {
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    v = v + (v >> 8);
    v = v + (v >> 16);
    v = v + (v >> 32);
    return v & 0xFFULL;
  };

  uint64_t loCount  = count32(x.lo);
  uint64_t hiCount  = count32(x.hi);

  uint128 r = {0, 0};
  // lane0: lo low 32 bits -> low byte of r.lo
  r.lo = (loCount & 0xFFULL);
  // lane1: lo high 32 bits -> low byte of r.lo at bits 32-39
  r.lo |= (loCount & 0xFFULL) << 8;
  // lane2: hi low 32 bits -> bits 64-71 of r.hi
  r.hi = (hiCount & 0xFFULL);
  // lane3: hi high 32 bits -> bits 96-103 of r.hi
  r.hi |= (hiCount & 0xFFULL) << 8;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for arbitrary lane widths (e.g., 64-bit lanes) while preserving parallelism?
- What are the potential pitfalls with endianness or portability across compilers for the approach above?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses branch-free 128-bit rotation, a common primitive in crypto and low-level optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values via two 64-bit halves
- Branchless selection with masks
- Cross-half rotation logic and edge cases (n=0, n=64, n=127)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, uint8_t n) {
  uint64_t n0 = n & 63;
  uint64_t m  = -((int)((n >> 6) & 1)); // 0 or -1
  uint64_t hi1 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo1 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t hi2 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo2 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (hi1 & ~m) | (hi2 & m);
  r.lo = (lo1 & ~m) | (lo2 & m);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How might you test for constant-time properties across inputs?
- How would you extend to a constant-time generic rotate for arbitrary bit-widths?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests precise bit trick mastery and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Bit hacks
- popcount and next permutation
- Undefined behavior on max value

## Code Example
```javascript
function nextBitPerm64(x) {
  const smallest = x & -x;
  const ripple = x + smallest;
  const ones = x ^ ripple;
  const shifted = (ones >> 2) / smallest;
  return ripple | shifted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 32-bit or arbitrary width?
- What are the performance implications on different CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of 64-bit bit reversals and cross-half manipulation without loops. It ensures ability to compose operations on 128-bit types.

## Key Concepts
- 64-bit bit reversal with masks
- 128-bit struct handling
- Non-branching composition

## Code Example
```javascript
// Outline only: reverse64 implemented with a sequence of mask/shifts
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1 | (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2 | (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  x = (x & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL) << 4 | (x >> 4) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  x = ((x & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL);
  x = (x << 48) | ((x & 0xffff) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0xffff);
  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Extend to 128-bit popcount-free reversal across 16-byte boundary
- Compare against a byte-by-byte reversal approach for throughput

## Why This Is Asked
Tests non-branching bit-twiddling and packing/unpacking across a mask, a common performance pattern in high-throughput code.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masks and order-preserving packing
- Non-branching compression via cascade masks
- Fixed-sequence bit-manipulation tricks

## Code Example
```javascript
function bitpack64(v, mask){
  let x = v & mask;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 1n)) & 0x5555555555555555n;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 2n)) & 0x3333333333333333n;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 8n)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 16n)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 32n)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFn;
  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support dynamic masks without recompilation?
- Compare performance with a loop-based approach on large data sets.
